<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mur (Mura) is a river about 450 kilometres long in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It rises in the Hohe Tauern in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, flows east through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, and then forms parts of the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a>. Its valley was the core of the medieval march that became Styria.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An earthquake originating in the Central Apennines was felt in Rome and Spoleto and damaged the basilica of San Paolo Fuori le <b>Mura</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-29">29 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Prekmurje, between the <b>Mura</b> river and the Hungarian border, had a Slovene-speaking majority but had long belonged to Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-prekmurje">Republic of Prekmurje</a></li></ul></section>
